00:26:41.409 --> 00:26:43.345
and most of them are
like, you know, normal.

00:26:43.345 --> 00:26:45.013
You know, you have a track that's called

00:26:45.013 --> 00:26:46.802
"The Rising Of The Shield Hero."

00:26:46.802 --> 00:26:47.635
- Of course.
- Of course.

00:26:47.635 --> 00:26:48.468
- The titular title.

00:26:48.468 --> 00:26:50.206
Of course, you know, you have like some,

00:26:50.206 --> 00:26:53.579
you know, isekai type track
called like "Game Start"

00:26:53.579 --> 00:26:54.412
and stuff like that.

00:26:54.412 --> 00:26:56.272
And then you go down
to like near the bottom

00:26:56.272 --> 00:26:58.032
and then there's a track
called like "Spirit Bomb"

00:26:58.032 --> 00:26:58.865
and I'm like, huh?

00:26:58.865 --> 00:27:01.463
Was that like a cheap
"Dragon Ball Z" reference?

00:27:01.463 --> 00:27:02.596
Probably is, it's Kevin, okay.

00:27:02.596 --> 00:27:05.064
We can get away with that.

00:27:05.064 --> 00:27:06.386
We can get away with that.

00:27:06.386 --> 00:27:08.186
And then you're just like,

00:27:08.186 --> 00:27:10.502
"Boom, Boom, Boom."

00:27:10.502 --> 00:27:12.360
(laughing)

00:27:12.360 --> 00:27:14.990
You're like, Kevin, are you
kind of running out names?

00:27:14.990 --> 00:27:16.819
And then you go down even further,

00:27:16.819 --> 00:27:18.999
and then there's a track
just called "Noms."

00:27:18.999 --> 00:27:21.249
(laughing)

00:27:23.635 --> 00:27:24.975
I listened to the track and I'm just like,

00:27:24.975 --> 00:27:27.920
this name has nothing to
do with the track at all.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:30.881
And then you go next
there's like "Mattyboi".

00:27:30.881 --> 00:27:31.964
- "Mattyboi".

00:27:33.118 --> 00:27:34.167
- Who's Mattyboi?

00:27:34.167 --> 00:27:35.060
Who's Mattyboi?

00:27:35.060 --> 00:27:36.302
I want to know.
- I can actually explain this.

00:27:36.302 --> 00:27:38.099
I can't explain the "Noms",

00:27:38.099 --> 00:27:39.324
I'm out on that one.

00:27:39.324 --> 00:27:40.321
(laughing)

00:27:40.321 --> 00:27:41.642
My bad on that one but-

00:27:41.642 --> 00:27:44.562
- That was the 2:00 AM chicken sandwich.

00:27:44.562 --> 00:27:47.091
- He was like, I could really
go for BK right now, "Noms"

00:27:47.091 --> 00:27:47.962
(laughing)

00:27:47.962 --> 00:27:49.057
- What was the first one you said it was?

00:27:49.057 --> 00:27:51.943
It was, oh "Boom, Boom, Boom".

00:27:51.943 --> 00:27:53.790
Have you watched "The Interview"?

00:27:53.790 --> 00:27:56.977
Like with Franco and Seth Rogan?

00:27:56.977 --> 00:27:57.850
- Yeah.

00:27:57.850 --> 00:27:59.481
- The one that like got so
many hacks by North Korea?

00:27:59.481 --> 00:28:00.326
- Yeah, yeah.

00:28:00.326 --> 00:28:03.812
- No, no, 'cause like when
they finally like shoot down

00:28:03.812 --> 00:28:04.831
Kim Jong Un in the movies,

00:28:04.831 --> 00:28:06.739
I spoiled that again.

00:28:06.739 --> 00:28:10.005
Fucking Franco's character
just goes, "Boom, Boom, Boom"

00:28:10.005 --> 00:28:12.601
and he like fires a tank
bullet into the helicopter

00:28:12.601 --> 00:28:15.308
and that's the reference.
- That's it?

00:28:15.308 --> 00:28:16.560
- That's it?
- That's it.

00:28:16.560 --> 00:28:17.921
- What's that got to
do with "Shield Hero?"

00:28:17.921 --> 00:28:19.421
- Fucking nothing.

00:28:20.865 --> 00:28:22.319
- Is it even like a booming track,

00:28:22.319 --> 00:28:23.719
it's just like?
- I don't know.

00:28:23.719 --> 00:28:24.571
I think so, yeah.

00:28:24.571 --> 00:28:26.347
No, it is a little bit.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:28:26.347 --> 00:28:27.748
- Like a battle thing on.

00:28:27.748 --> 00:28:29.929
- Checklist, it was like 50 plus tracks.

00:28:29.929 --> 00:28:32.682
- [Kevin] That's the whole idea, yeah.

00:28:32.682 --> 00:28:35.039
- That seems like excessive almost.

00:28:35.039 --> 00:28:38.247
- Well, I mean, cause it
was 25 or 26 episodes,

00:28:38.247 --> 00:28:39.545
so that required.

00:28:39.545 --> 00:28:42.870
So basically what happens,
as I'm sure you all know,

00:28:42.870 --> 00:28:44.481
but just for clarity and
all that sort of thing.

00:28:44.481 --> 00:28:46.316
So there's normally cores.

00:28:46.316 --> 00:28:49.354
So one core, two cores.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:28:49.354 --> 00:28:51.235
- Core?
- Like a season kind of.

00:28:51.235 --> 00:28:52.068
- Oh, oh, oh.

00:28:52.068 --> 00:28:53.241
- So one core is 12 or 13 episodes.

00:28:53.241 --> 00:28:54.074
- Oh okay.

00:28:54.074 --> 00:28:55.063
- If it's two core,

00:28:55.063 --> 00:28:56.810
it's like 24, 25, 26 episodes

00:28:56.810 --> 00:28:58.357
or something along that length.

00:28:58.357 --> 00:29:02.364
And so for example "Made In
Abyss" season one of course

00:29:02.364 --> 00:29:04.878
is like 13 episodes so it's-

00:29:04.878 --> 00:29:06.306
- One core.
- All right, all right.

00:29:06.306 --> 00:29:08.504
- But "Shield Hero" had 26
episode, you do two core.

00:29:08.504 --> 00:29:09.873
So normally for two cores,

00:29:09.873 --> 00:29:11.578
you don't double the music,

00:29:11.578 --> 00:29:13.179
but you probably play 1.5 times it.

00:29:13.179 --> 00:29:14.012
- Right.
- Okay.

00:29:14.012 --> 00:29:17.345
- So for "Made in Abyss" the season one,

00:29:20.501 --> 00:29:22.934
I think the soundtrack comes
in at one hour and 52 minutes,

00:29:22.934 --> 00:29:24.029
something like that.
- Right.

00:29:24.029 --> 00:29:26.017
- And I think it's about,

00:29:26.017 --> 00:29:27.658
I think that was actually
about 50 something tracks

00:29:27.658 --> 00:29:29.601
or like maybe 40, mid 40 tracks.

00:29:29.601 --> 00:29:30.560
- Jesus.
- Yeah.

00:29:30.560 --> 00:29:31.438
- But then "Shield Hero" yeah,

00:29:31.438 --> 00:29:33.827
it was about 60, 62 tracks

00:29:33.827 --> 00:29:35.622
and it was about two hours
and 50 minutes of music.

00:29:35.622 --> 00:29:37.148
- And is that like left up to you,

00:29:37.148 --> 00:29:38.940
do you decide how many
tracks you're making?

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:39.966
- No actually.
- Or are they like,

00:29:39.966 --> 00:29:41.240
hey, we need 62 tracks,

00:29:41.240 --> 00:29:42.160
make them all sound different.

00:29:42.160 --> 00:29:43.282
- It's actually pretty specific.

00:29:43.282 --> 00:29:44.725
So what basically happens is

00:29:44.725 --> 00:29:46.883
when you get commissioned
to write anime music,

00:29:46.883 --> 00:29:49.722
you get just basically
a massive Excel sheet

00:29:49.722 --> 00:29:51.577
called the music order.
- Yeah.

00:29:51.577 --> 00:29:52.455
- So when you get the music order,

00:29:52.455 --> 00:29:54.543
it's basically, yeah just like

00:29:54.543 --> 00:29:56.994
it'll have like M1, like music one,

00:29:56.994 --> 00:29:58.456
and then it'll say like main theme.

00:29:58.456 --> 00:29:59.686
And then like M2 will be like

00:29:59.686 --> 00:30:01.034
main theme happy version.

00:30:01.034 --> 00:30:02.791
And then like M3 three will be

00:30:02.791 --> 00:30:04.298
main theme dark version or something.

00:30:04.298 --> 00:30:05.536
- Right, right, right.

00:30:05.536 --> 00:30:07.731
- M4, main theme hero version
or something like that.

00:30:07.731 --> 00:30:08.564
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:30:08.564 --> 00:30:10.286
- Just quite generic
sort of like descriptions

00:30:10.286 --> 00:30:11.618
and they might have some explanation

00:30:11.618 --> 00:30:12.680
or they might have some, you know,

00:30:12.680 --> 00:30:14.052
some visual images and stuff like that.

00:30:14.052 --> 00:30:15.371
So you actually get a lot of freedom,

00:30:15.371 --> 00:30:17.047
which I really, really like.
- Yeah.

00:30:17.047 --> 00:30:19.682
- So if you think about how like,

00:30:19.682 --> 00:30:21.646
'cause they'll have all that sort of thing

00:30:21.646 --> 00:30:23.711
and then maybe once you get
to like M20 it'll be like,

00:30:23.711 --> 00:30:24.941
battle one speed battle,

00:30:24.941 --> 00:30:27.160
or like battle two, like serious battle.

00:30:27.160 --> 00:30:28.552
- That's literally,
that's all they give you?

00:30:28.552 --> 00:30:30.519
- Sometimes, I mean, we can
get very specific as well

00:30:30.519 --> 00:30:32.601
but like it'll start
from that sort of like

00:30:32.601 --> 00:30:34.417
slightly more abstract sort of approach

00:30:34.417 --> 00:30:35.888
which actually really, really cool

00:30:35.888 --> 00:30:37.884
because that way you
actually do have a lot of,

00:30:37.884 --> 00:30:39.889
kind of, like I said, freedom

00:30:39.889 --> 00:30:41.834
to actually just try to
explore different ideas

00:30:41.834 --> 00:30:43.740
as just like-

00:30:43.740 --> 00:30:45.789
- As you get the freedom
to call or track "Noms".

00:30:45.789 --> 00:30:47.144
(laughing)

00:30:47.144 --> 00:30:50.706
- I guess the track name
doesn't really matter, right?

00:30:50.706 --> 00:30:52.982
- When you're writing it,

00:30:52.982 --> 00:30:54.856
like my process on this has
changed like a little bit

00:30:54.856 --> 00:30:56.090
since "Shield Hero" admittedly

00:30:56.090 --> 00:30:57.448
so I probably wouldn't do that again,

00:30:57.448 --> 00:31:00.564
but it was-
- There was a process?

00:31:00.564 --> 00:31:02.481
- You know, eventually.

00:31:03.392 --> 00:31:05.809
Well, so when you're writing,

00:31:07.859 --> 00:31:11.039
what I like to do is try
and match a lot of like

00:31:11.039 --> 00:31:12.786
sort of the texture stuff I
was talking about earlier.

00:31:12.786 --> 00:31:13.619
- Yeah.

00:31:13.619 --> 00:31:15.789
- I love trying to match
texture to the actual visual

00:31:15.789 --> 00:31:18.718
sort of palette, if you will.

00:31:18.718 --> 00:31:20.509
Best way, like one of
the most sort of like

00:31:20.509 --> 00:31:22.500
sort of basic examples or
classic examples I can give

00:31:22.500 --> 00:31:24.988
is in "Made In Abyss" obviously like

00:31:24.988 --> 00:31:27.867
it's quite a small amount
of like kind of characters

00:31:27.867 --> 00:31:30.081
going to like huge like cavernous abyss

00:31:30.081 --> 00:31:30.914
and started exploring.

00:31:30.914 --> 00:31:31.935
And also you see in the visuals,

00:31:31.935 --> 00:31:34.159
there's normally a lot of wide shots

00:31:34.159 --> 00:31:35.680
where you just see kind of the-

00:31:35.680 --> 00:31:37.208
- The scale.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:31:37.208 --> 00:31:38.947
- It just goes like super far.

00:31:38.947 --> 00:31:41.632
And so like the idea of
trying to actually depict

00:31:41.632 --> 00:31:45.261
that visual sort of stimuli in music

00:31:45.261 --> 00:31:46.473
is quite appealing to me.

00:31:46.473 --> 00:31:48.894
It's sort of, yeah, almost like a,

00:31:48.894 --> 00:31:50.648
it's called like word painting.

00:31:50.648 --> 00:31:51.481
Which is basically like,

00:31:51.481 --> 00:31:52.940
let's say you have lyrics and say,

00:31:52.940 --> 00:31:54.164
"I am going to rise up",

00:31:54.164 --> 00:31:55.017
and then the melody goes,

00:31:55.017 --> 00:31:56.130
"I am going to rise up."

00:31:56.130 --> 00:31:59.052
It's like literally rising with the-

00:31:59.052 --> 00:32:01.093
- Like rising with the shield right.

00:32:01.093 --> 00:32:03.025
- Rising with the shield.

00:32:03.025 --> 00:32:04.024
- Well actually, 'cause in the main theme,

00:32:04.024 --> 00:32:06.510
it's like the violin
start part of the start

00:32:06.510 --> 00:32:07.960
does actually rise up.
- Right.

00:32:07.960 --> 00:32:09.540
- And again, it's like,
it sounds so basic,

00:32:09.540 --> 00:32:11.508
but it is good fun.
- Yeah.

00:32:11.508 --> 00:32:14.969
- A lot of the like chord
stuff in "Made In Abyss"

00:32:14.969 --> 00:32:16.121
more so like you saw that accession

00:32:16.121 --> 00:32:17.608
so you hear a lot of like,

00:32:17.608 --> 00:32:19.330
it's normally going down or up,

00:32:19.330 --> 00:32:23.036
but like the whole idea of
like things rising and falling

00:32:23.036 --> 00:32:24.399
as best as possible.

00:32:24.399 --> 00:32:27.206
- So like, one thing I've
always wondered is pretty much

00:32:27.206 --> 00:32:30.137
how you like blew up onto the scene.

00:32:30.137 --> 00:32:34.003
'Cause I think like
you blew up immediately

00:32:34.003 --> 00:32:35.237
in terms of like sound,

00:32:35.237 --> 00:32:36.070
in terms of being a composer,

00:32:36.070 --> 00:32:37.863
which is like, I find it's really rare,

00:32:37.863 --> 00:32:40.360
but I think a big part of that was just

00:32:40.360 --> 00:32:43.211
that opening scene in "Made In Abyss"

00:32:43.211 --> 00:32:45.134
where it was just like the instrumental

00:32:45.134 --> 00:32:46.965
with like the vocals as well,

00:32:46.965 --> 00:32:48.173
and it was just no dialogue

00:32:48.173 --> 00:32:52.060
it was just like a montage
just using this opening track.

00:32:52.060 --> 00:32:54.054
And it just like immediately,

00:32:54.054 --> 00:32:56.404
it not only did it sell you on the show,

00:32:56.404 --> 00:32:59.599
but it just like put
your work onto the map.

00:32:59.599 --> 00:33:01.024
Like how did that come about?

00:33:01.024 --> 00:33:04.746
Was that planned or was that
just like a happy coincidence?

00:33:04.746 --> 00:33:07.194
- I was like totally lucky.

00:33:07.194 --> 00:33:09.893
And I'm glad I was like almost

00:33:09.893 --> 00:33:11.455
'cause I had a different track.

00:33:11.455 --> 00:33:13.694
I had had a much more subtle
track in mind for that.

00:33:13.694 --> 00:33:15.495
- [Garnt] Right.

00:33:15.495 --> 00:33:16.559
- Called "Swings And Roundabouts."

00:33:16.559 --> 00:33:18.784
That was like kind of my
original idea for that theme.

00:33:18.784 --> 00:33:19.655
So it was supposed to be,

00:33:19.655 --> 00:33:20.909
oh, let's do these nice kind of

00:33:20.909 --> 00:33:22.089
really cool textures and stuff

00:33:22.089 --> 00:33:24.732
as the light kind comes over.

00:33:24.732 --> 00:33:27.613
But then the sound
director and the director

00:33:27.613 --> 00:33:29.421
decided differently to kind of,

00:33:29.421 --> 00:33:30.925
'cause you don't write these,

00:33:30.925 --> 00:33:32.668
unless it's a movie or
it's a specific scene

00:33:32.668 --> 00:33:34.250
that's being requested to be written

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:35.762
synchronized to picture,

00:33:35.762 --> 00:33:37.705
all the music is written
what's called free.

00:33:37.705 --> 00:33:39.911
- Right.
- And then it's synced later.

00:33:39.911 --> 00:33:40.948
- Oh.

00:33:40.948 --> 00:33:43.253
So that wasn't meant to be synced at all?

00:33:43.253 --> 00:33:46.581
- Originally like my like vision
of it was to be different,

00:33:46.581 --> 00:33:49.831
but the director and the sound director

00:33:51.779 --> 00:33:53.147
obviously had a pretty deep discussion,

00:33:53.147 --> 00:33:55.365
they decided to put that song there

00:33:55.365 --> 00:33:57.201
and even better they
decided to put it there

00:33:57.201 --> 00:33:59.432
with minimal dialogue and sound effects

00:33:59.432 --> 00:34:01.729
which is ridiculously like lucky.

00:34:01.729 --> 00:34:03.881
And so just to, you know,

00:34:03.881 --> 00:34:05.631
I guess just to like,

00:34:07.056 --> 00:34:09.499
how do you even explain this?

00:34:09.499 --> 00:34:13.104
To see that happen and
to be so happily wrong

00:34:13.104 --> 00:34:14.698
about like what my vision was for that.

00:34:14.698 --> 00:34:15.531
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:34:15.531 --> 00:34:16.364
- I think I was just like,

00:34:16.364 --> 00:34:17.692
oh man, thank God people
are smarter than me.

00:34:17.692 --> 00:34:20.767
But like, cause that song was written

00:34:20.767 --> 00:34:24.235
just as sort of kind of
almost just like another,

00:34:24.235 --> 00:34:26.534
like trying to make another
palette for the soundtrack.

00:34:26.534 --> 00:34:28.105
Because when you start it

00:34:28.105 --> 00:34:29.479
you just kind of going through like

00:34:29.479 --> 00:34:30.788
point after point after point.

00:34:30.788 --> 00:34:31.636
It's like, okay,

00:34:31.636 --> 00:34:33.326
well let's just try and
write a piece of music

00:34:33.326 --> 00:34:34.541
based off this manga panel,

00:34:34.541 --> 00:34:36.510
or let's write a piece of music
based off this description

00:34:36.510 --> 00:34:37.409
or this idea, you know,

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:40.000
obviously like reading
through the content and stuff.

00:34:40.000 --> 00:34:41.045
- Oh right, so I was thinking like,

00:34:41.045 --> 00:34:42.455
oh yeah, what do they give you?

00:34:42.455 --> 00:34:43.734
I'm like, oh yeah, you
can just go to the manga

00:34:43.734 --> 00:34:45.579
most of the time, right?
- Yeah.

00:34:45.579 --> 00:34:47.611
Manga or in "Tower Of God"
you got manhwa as well.

00:34:47.611 --> 00:34:48.768
- Yeah, yeah.

00:34:48.768 --> 00:34:51.287
- That, it really is helpful.

00:34:51.287 --> 00:34:52.876
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:34:52.876 --> 00:34:55.011
- Knowing the layout.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:34:55.011 --> 00:34:55.876
- It's super helpful.

00:34:55.876 --> 00:35:00.293
And so just when you're able
to like kind of look at,

00:35:01.179 --> 00:35:02.491
essentially you can just
see the entire story

00:35:02.491 --> 00:35:03.324
kind of pan out.

00:35:03.324 --> 00:35:04.157
Obviously you're given

00:35:04.157 --> 00:35:05.545
like anime specific materials as well,

00:35:05.545 --> 00:35:07.098
which is super, super helpful.

00:35:07.098 --> 00:35:09.825
But yeah, when you're just
trying to find something

00:35:09.825 --> 00:35:12.248
that actually kind of hopefully,

00:35:12.248 --> 00:35:14.488
like what's the best word?

00:35:14.488 --> 00:35:18.046
Respects the content as well.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:35:18.046 --> 00:35:21.783
- You know, you're adding an audio element

00:35:21.783 --> 00:35:23.070
that was not there before,

00:35:23.070 --> 00:35:24.298
whereas the manga might be there before

00:35:24.298 --> 00:35:25.682
so there might be reference,

00:35:25.682 --> 00:35:26.814
you know, people might
be able to reference

00:35:26.814 --> 00:35:29.161
the anime to the manga and say, okay,

00:35:29.161 --> 00:35:30.290
well this is similar, this is different,

00:35:30.290 --> 00:35:31.123
something like that.

00:35:31.123 --> 00:35:34.197
The music never existed so.

00:35:34.197 --> 00:35:36.021
It's both freeing and
also kind of terrifying

00:35:36.021 --> 00:35:37.875
to, you know, potentially mess it up.

00:35:37.875 --> 00:35:38.708
- Yeah, that's the thing.

00:35:38.708 --> 00:35:39.541
'Cause like when I saw that scene,

00:35:39.541 --> 00:35:41.884
I was like, this is so perfect
for this opening scene,

00:35:41.884 --> 00:35:45.233
