how you you're doing takes
or interviews and stuff

01:31:46.357 --> 01:31:47.655
and then you're like, "Have we got it?

01:31:47.655 --> 01:31:48.594
"Do we need to go again?"

01:31:48.594 --> 01:31:49.838
Have like that sort of,

01:31:49.838 --> 01:31:50.768
what would be the word?

01:31:50.768 --> 01:31:53.158
Nervousness about, okay, co
we actually have the content?

01:31:53.158 --> 01:31:54.455
Or do we not have the content,

01:31:54.455 --> 01:31:55.467
do we need to go again?

01:31:55.467 --> 01:31:58.238
And that-
- Right, it's important.

01:31:58.238 --> 01:31:59.071
- Yeah.

01:31:59.071 --> 01:32:01.101
But if you are filming it and you're also,

01:32:01.101 --> 01:32:02.051
you know, in the shot,

01:32:02.051 --> 01:32:05.608
then you've gotta go back
and and forth in that mind.

01:32:05.608 --> 01:32:08.602
But having someone behind the camera,

01:32:08.602 --> 01:32:10.774
or in this case, in the recording booth,

01:32:10.774 --> 01:32:13.860
saying, "No, we got it,
it's chilled let's move on."

01:32:13.860 --> 01:32:15.290
That kind of moves grease-

01:32:15.290 --> 01:32:17.389
- It's like nice reinforcement.

01:32:17.389 --> 01:32:20.222
- It's like, I guess, they should,

01:32:21.607 --> 01:32:23.493
but like the cameraman,

01:32:23.493 --> 01:32:24.971
he's like getting the shot,

01:32:24.971 --> 01:32:27.529
but he doesn't necessarily give a shit

01:32:27.529 --> 01:32:30.472
if it doesn't look great
sometimes, it depends.

01:32:30.472 --> 01:32:33.061
- Okay, I would avoid that.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:32:33.061 --> 01:32:33.894
- I would recommend-

01:32:33.894 --> 01:32:35.852
- And it's also, sometimes they don't know

01:32:35.852 --> 01:32:38.348
what kind of shot that you want as like,

01:32:38.348 --> 01:32:40.155
normally with a director-

01:32:40.155 --> 01:32:41.161
- They don't have as much investment

01:32:41.161 --> 01:32:43.226
as like say someone you
hire to be invested.

01:32:43.226 --> 01:32:46.128
- Yeah, well, I mean, if you're
hiring a session producer,

01:32:46.128 --> 01:32:48.002
they should, hopefully be invested.

01:32:48.002 --> 01:32:49.007
- So you're hiring someone who's like

01:32:49.007 --> 01:32:51.189
his job is to be as invested as you.

01:32:51.189 --> 01:32:52.502
- Right.
- Yes, yeah.

01:32:52.502 --> 01:32:54.469
- But obviously not everyone
will be as invested as you.

01:32:54.469 --> 01:32:57.428
- Because otherwise he's
just a guy at the booth.

01:32:57.428 --> 01:32:58.387
- No, I do see where
you're coming from now.

01:32:58.387 --> 01:33:01.284
So there are times when
someone is just saying,

01:33:01.284 --> 01:33:03.978
okay, have we got the,
you know, the standard?

01:33:03.978 --> 01:33:04.820
- They'll be like, "Yeah, we got it."

01:33:04.820 --> 01:33:05.873
- Yeah, we got it.
- Right.

01:33:05.873 --> 01:33:07.493
- And then you go back and
you're like, you didn't have it.

01:33:07.493 --> 01:33:11.599
- No, but then that would
maybe where experience kicks in

01:33:11.599 --> 01:33:13.181
and you say, no, we're gonna go again.

01:33:13.181 --> 01:33:14.014
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.
- Yeah.

01:33:14.014 --> 01:33:14.847
- At the end of the day,

01:33:14.847 --> 01:33:15.680
you're kind of driving the train.

01:33:15.680 --> 01:33:17.340
Like people are really supporting you,

01:33:17.340 --> 01:33:18.421
people are there doing their job,

01:33:18.421 --> 01:33:21.501
but between you and maybe
like the music supervisor

01:33:21.501 --> 01:33:22.334
or something like that,

01:33:22.334 --> 01:33:23.711
you guys are driving that train

01:33:23.711 --> 01:33:24.831
so you've gotta be like on it.

01:33:24.831 --> 01:33:27.222
And this is where it's
not just a physical job,

01:33:27.222 --> 01:33:29.092
it's also super mental

01:33:29.092 --> 01:33:31.059
because you've gotta focus on not only,

01:33:31.059 --> 01:33:32.087
especially if you're conducting,

01:33:32.087 --> 01:33:33.455
you got the physicality of making sure,

01:33:33.455 --> 01:33:34.568
okay, cue that person in,

01:33:34.568 --> 01:33:35.589
keep on going like that.

01:33:35.589 --> 01:33:38.634
You come in, you come in,
be three, blah, blah, blah.

01:33:38.634 --> 01:33:40.607
You know, so you're cuing people

01:33:40.607 --> 01:33:41.786
even though they can do it.

01:33:41.786 --> 01:33:44.845
But you know, through the
physical act of conducting,

01:33:44.845 --> 01:33:45.860
it's not just about keeping tempo,

01:33:45.860 --> 01:33:47.814
which to be fair, everyone's
got a click in their ear

01:33:47.814 --> 01:33:49.619
so it doesn't really matter too much.

01:33:49.619 --> 01:33:52.510
But what it's about is coaxing
more performance out of it,

01:33:52.510 --> 01:33:54.503
"More, more, more."

01:33:54.503 --> 01:33:55.336
- Like, you're basically just like

01:33:55.336 --> 01:33:57.726
micromanaging everyone, right?
- Yeah, yeah.

01:33:57.726 --> 01:33:58.751
Hopefully in like in a positive way.

01:33:58.751 --> 01:33:59.753
- Yeah, yeah, yeah

01:33:59.753 --> 01:34:00.783
- But it's like, for example,

01:34:00.783 --> 01:34:02.142
if I'm going like this to you,

01:34:02.142 --> 01:34:04.405
I'm like, okay, sing, sing, sing, sing.

01:34:04.405 --> 01:34:07.154
And then okay, on that last
one then it's like sing more.

01:34:07.154 --> 01:34:09.219
- Yeah.
- Right.

01:34:09.219 --> 01:34:10.052
- Sing.

01:34:10.943 --> 01:34:12.869
- Do it again.

01:34:12.869 --> 01:34:14.025
- Sounds a bit constipated,

01:34:14.025 --> 01:34:15.846
but at the same time it's like, okay,

01:34:15.846 --> 01:34:18.990
that should hopefully coax
a little bit more, you know,

01:34:18.990 --> 01:34:20.794
you might push a little
harder or something like that.

01:34:20.794 --> 01:34:22.692
So if you're doing that,

01:34:22.692 --> 01:34:24.290
at the same time, you're also
listening to everyone else

01:34:24.290 --> 01:34:26.216
in your headphones or like in the room,

01:34:26.216 --> 01:34:28.412
making sure that everyone else
is playing at the same time.

01:34:28.412 --> 01:34:30.472
So you're doing that over and over again

01:34:30.472 --> 01:34:31.862
for nine hours a day.
- Yeah.

01:34:31.862 --> 01:34:35.828
- So like you take everything
you just described,

01:34:35.828 --> 01:34:37.836
how many more layers of difficulty

01:34:37.836 --> 01:34:40.041
has been added on with COVID?

01:34:40.041 --> 01:34:40.874
- I was gonna ask this.

01:34:40.874 --> 01:34:42.955
- And having to do things remotely.

01:34:42.955 --> 01:34:45.650
'Cause that sounds like
a nightmare in person.

01:34:45.650 --> 01:34:47.084
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:34:47.084 --> 01:34:49.055
- Yeah, there's a reason that

01:34:49.055 --> 01:34:50.963
if you see me like in the room,

01:34:50.963 --> 01:34:52.186
I'm like a sweaty mess

01:34:52.186 --> 01:34:54.633
'cause it is like super intensive.

01:34:54.633 --> 01:34:56.216
In the COVID times,

01:34:58.675 --> 01:35:00.889
it's been interesting because
depending on where you are,

01:35:00.889 --> 01:35:02.298
it's actually a little more convenient

01:35:02.298 --> 01:35:03.769
in terms of the time difference

01:35:03.769 --> 01:35:04.799
'cause obviously Australia is like

01:35:04.799 --> 01:35:06.900
so far in front of everywhere, so.

01:35:06.900 --> 01:35:08.949
If you're recording in
America it's quite nice

01:35:08.949 --> 01:35:10.918
'cause you know, it's sort of
like an early morning thing

01:35:10.918 --> 01:35:12.813
and I became a bit of an
early riser during COVID,

01:35:12.813 --> 01:35:14.635
at least for a time until suddenly

01:35:14.635 --> 01:35:16.651
I was very not an early riser.

01:35:16.651 --> 01:35:18.909
But in Europe it's kind of interesting.

01:35:18.909 --> 01:35:22.641
We did a lot of recording
in Prague, some Budapest-

01:35:22.641 --> 01:35:24.459
- During the pandemic?
- During the pandemic

01:35:24.459 --> 01:35:26.672
and for an upcoming project,

01:35:26.672 --> 01:35:28.701
we did a little recording
in America as well.

01:35:28.701 --> 01:35:29.534
- Right.

01:35:29.534 --> 01:35:33.701
- But yeah, normally for
Europe it's normally like

01:35:35.365 --> 01:35:37.177
an Australian nighttime sort of recording.

01:35:37.177 --> 01:35:39.392
So you kinda of sit down at like

01:35:39.392 --> 01:35:40.389
seven, eight o'clock at night

01:35:40.389 --> 01:35:41.887
and then you do six, seven hours.

01:35:41.887 --> 01:35:43.583
So you kind of finish
like two, three o'clock

01:35:43.583 --> 01:35:44.416
in the morning.

01:35:44.416 --> 01:35:46.039
- So you are on call.
- Yeah.

01:35:46.039 --> 01:35:48.864
- With people who are just in a big hall?

01:35:48.864 --> 01:35:50.405
- Yeah, so basically just stream the audio

01:35:50.405 --> 01:35:51.705
on Audiomovers and Zoom.

01:35:51.705 --> 01:35:52.857
- Wow.
- Yeah.

01:35:52.857 --> 01:35:54.945
It's actually, to be honest,
like actually not bad.

01:35:54.945 --> 01:35:57.055
- Really?
- But what if it like lags

01:35:57.055 --> 01:36:00.200
halfway through like the recording, right?

01:36:00.200 --> 01:36:02.176
- Cause like fucking
classic Australian internet

01:36:02.176 --> 01:36:04.472
just crashes.
- Yeah, I was about to say,

01:36:04.472 --> 01:36:07.929
I'm like, it must be shit.
- Yeah, it's like, cause yeah.

01:36:07.929 --> 01:36:08.780
- Oh Kevin's connecting.

01:36:08.780 --> 01:36:10.581
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

01:36:10.581 --> 01:36:15.067
- It's like, why is our
conductor in like 480p?

01:36:15.067 --> 01:36:16.316
I can barely see him.

01:36:16.316 --> 01:36:18.279
- At that point you're not
conducting unfortunately.

01:36:18.279 --> 01:36:19.146
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:36:19.146 --> 01:36:20.037
- That was what was really nice about

01:36:20.037 --> 01:36:22.002
like going back to Sydney
for a couple of projects.

01:36:22.002 --> 01:36:23.377
Sorry, going back to Sydney,

01:36:23.377 --> 01:36:25.728
going to Sydney for a couple of projects

01:36:25.728 --> 01:36:26.658
'cause like, yeah,

01:36:26.658 --> 01:36:27.902
Melbourne Sydney is
like an hour and a half

01:36:27.902 --> 01:36:30.406
on the plane and stuff
and that's pretty cool.

01:36:30.406 --> 01:36:34.837
But yeah, to finally get back
to conducting for "Star Wars"

01:36:34.837 --> 01:36:35.890
was really cool,

01:36:35.890 --> 01:36:37.050
'cause that was the
first time I'd done it.

01:36:37.050 --> 01:36:40.967
I forgot, 'cause the
muscles just like go away.

01:36:42.972 --> 01:36:43.855
- To conduct?

01:36:43.855 --> 01:36:46.432
- Yeah, cause like try doing this

01:36:46.432 --> 01:36:49.786
for like nine hours.
- Right, yah.

01:36:49.786 --> 01:36:51.274
- Like just the right arm.
- Just your right arm.

01:36:51.274 --> 01:36:52.417
(laughing)

01:36:52.417 --> 01:36:53.808
- You're like Quagmire, like.

01:36:53.808 --> 01:36:55.080
(laughing)

01:36:55.080 --> 01:36:55.950
- It's like what's up with your arm?

01:36:55.950 --> 01:36:57.823
I'm a conductor, I swear I'm a conductor.

01:36:57.823 --> 01:36:58.656
- We were talking about

01:36:58.656 --> 01:36:59.805
carpal tunnel and "Jump King" before.

01:36:59.805 --> 01:37:01.920
It's like that but for your entire arm.

01:37:01.920 --> 01:37:03.790
Just like that.

01:37:03.790 --> 01:37:04.623
- [Lady In Background] It's fine Garnt,

01:37:04.623 --> 01:37:05.860
he's left headed so they balance.

01:37:05.860 --> 01:37:08.110
(laughing)

01:37:09.793 --> 01:37:10.626
- It balances out.

01:37:10.626 --> 01:37:11.626
- Oh my God.

01:37:13.360 --> 01:37:15.829
- Just post pandemic, and
he's just arms are huge.

01:37:15.829 --> 01:37:18.435
- Perfectly balanced as
all things should be.

01:37:18.435 --> 01:37:20.685
(laughing)

01:37:23.123 --> 01:37:25.626
Yeah, I forgot how like
physically intensive it was.

01:37:25.626 --> 01:37:26.657
So I was just sat like.

01:37:26.657 --> 01:37:29.356
'Cause what you're normally meant to do

01:37:29.356 --> 01:37:30.653
is like maybe be a little bit reserved

01:37:30.653 --> 01:37:31.735
in like moving your arms and stuff.

01:37:31.735 --> 01:37:32.905
- You can't help yourself?
- Yeah.

01:37:32.905 --> 01:37:34.398
- But like you're getting into it,

01:37:34.398 --> 01:37:35.961
fuck this up, this is fucking good.

01:37:35.961 --> 01:37:37.344
That sort of thing and
then all of a sudden

01:37:37.344 --> 01:37:39.625
you're moving your entire
body doing like Zumba

01:37:39.625 --> 01:37:41.777
for like quite a few hours.

01:37:41.777 --> 01:37:42.699
- Jesus.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:37:42.699 --> 01:37:43.537
- It's a little interesting.

01:37:43.537 --> 01:37:44.647
- Sounds really intense.
- Yeah.

01:37:44.647 --> 01:37:46.463
- So like speaking of, oh, sorry.

01:37:46.463 --> 01:37:48.058
- No, no, I have nothing of value to say.

01:37:48.058 --> 01:37:50.877
(laughing)

01:37:50.877 --> 01:37:52.147
- Man's literally talked
for an hour and a half

01:37:52.147 --> 01:37:54.953
and he was like, that was
all just bants bro, like.

01:37:54.953 --> 01:37:56.251
- Literally just talking out of my ass,

01:37:56.251 --> 01:37:57.511
none of that was real.

01:37:57.511 --> 01:37:59.039
- Literally talking to-
- Time to wake up.

01:37:59.039 --> 01:38:01.058
- Literally talking to
like an anime composer,

01:38:01.058 --> 01:38:02.858
composed for some of the bigger shows

01:38:02.858 --> 01:38:04.028
in like the past few years,

01:38:04.028 --> 01:38:06.812
"I literally have
nothing of value to say."

01:38:06.812 --> 01:38:08.937
(laughing)

01:38:08.937 --> 01:38:10.778
- So "Star Wars".
- Yeah.

01:38:10.778 --> 01:38:13.875
- How was that like?
- Really cool.

01:38:13.875 --> 01:38:16.788
- How was it when like the
news came in to be like,

01:38:16.788 --> 01:38:20.620
can you compose a piece of like
music for "Star Wars" media?

01:38:20.620 --> 01:38:21.453
Cause I'm assuming like-

01:38:21.453 --> 01:38:22.755
- Well it's a great honor.

01:38:22.755 --> 01:38:26.922
You know, obviously it has
the history of history.

01:38:29.847 --> 01:38:33.579
So "Star Wars: Visions"
is an animated series

01:38:33.579 --> 01:38:35.406
based in the universe "Star Wars"

01:38:35.406 --> 01:38:37.681
and I did one of those episodes,

01:38:37.681 --> 01:38:38.700
it's nine episode.

01:38:38.700 --> 01:38:41.879
Did the episode four
called "Village Bride".

01:38:41.879 --> 01:38:45.506
And what I really liked about it is that

01:38:45.506 --> 01:38:48.137
"Star Wars: Visions", our episode,

01:38:48.137 --> 01:38:50.943
explored like some really
cool like spiritual

01:38:50.943 --> 01:38:52.443
and biblical show.

01:38:53.824 --> 01:38:54.770
- I was waiting for that one.

01:38:54.770 --> 01:38:56.310
I was like, where is it, where is it.

01:38:56.310 --> 01:38:58.181
(laughing)

01:38:58.181 --> 01:39:01.791
- No, it explored some really
nice concepts with the force,

01:39:01.791 --> 01:39:05.030
which was just, I mean,
I grew up watching that.

01:39:05.030 --> 01:39:06.187
- [Garnt] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:39:06.187 --> 01:39:07.616
- I watched the original
trilogy with my dad,

01:39:07.616 --> 01:39:10.468
then my dad and I watched the prequels.

01:39:10.468 --> 01:39:12.555
Then I did watch the
sequels as well, of course.

01:39:12.555 --> 01:39:16.638
But yeah, it was really
nice to sort of have like

01:39:18.743 --> 01:39:21.417
one hand in like sort
of that aesthetic world,

01:39:21.417 --> 01:39:23.978
at the same time we're exploring

01:39:23.978 --> 01:39:25.247
certain like spiritual aspects,

01:39:25.247 --> 01:39:27.174
especially considering like the backdrop

01:39:27.174 --> 01:39:30.230
is very like sort of Kyoto esque.

01:39:30.230 --> 01:39:31.143
- [Garnt] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:39:31.143 --> 01:39:34.393
So like to have like the ability to use

01:39:36.107 --> 01:39:37.509
like traditional Japanese instruments.

01:39:37.509 --> 01:39:39.019
We used a Sho,

01:39:39.019 --> 01:39:39.902
we used Koto,

01:39:39.902 --> 01:39:40.940
we used Shakuhachi,

01:39:40.940 --> 01:39:43.240
and we did a Shinobue as well.
- Right.

01:39:43.240 --> 01:39:44.580
- And so we recorded those in Tokyo,

01:39:44.580 --> 01:39:46.744
recorded orchestra in Sydney,

01:39:46.744 --> 01:39:50.108
put 'em all together-
- It's crazy.

01:39:50.108 --> 01:39:52.275
- Especially the Sho is a-

01:39:53.802 --> 01:39:54.635
- Which one is that?

01:39:54.635 --> 01:39:56.718
- Sho is the mouth organ.

01:39:58.586 --> 01:40:01.551
I mean, I'm very, very
oversimplifying it to say that-

01:40:01.551 --> 01:40:03.271
- I've never heard of this.

01:40:03.271 --> 01:40:04.521
- Yeah, I know.

01:40:05.641 --> 01:40:07.364
And then when Kevin
said it's a mouth organ,

01:40:07.364 --> 01:40:09.604
I was this close to
being like, no, it's not.

01:40:09.604 --> 01:40:11.607
(laughing)

01:40:11.607 --> 01:40:16.607
- So you blow into like a
giant amount sort of like,

01:40:16.625 --> 01:40:18.111
giant amount, thanks Kevin.

01:40:18.111 --> 01:40:19.671
- Giant amount, a large amount.

01:40:19.671 --> 01:40:23.956
- A large amount of bamboo
pipes and it creates-

01:40:23.956 --> 01:40:24.817
- You've definitely seen it before.

01:40:24.817 --> 01:40:26.420
- Yeah.
- Right.

01:40:26.420 --> 01:40:29.521
- It's part of a temple culture a lot.

01:40:29.521 --> 01:40:30.482
It's like, (humming)

01:40:30.482 --> 01:40:32.792
I can't-
- right, right, right.

01:40:32.792 --> 01:40:33.685
- I mean, you would've heard it,

01:40:33.685 --> 01:40:34.747
you would've absolutely heard it.

01:40:34.747 --> 01:40:39.247
But it's like 12 to 14 sort
of individual little pipes

01:40:41.321 --> 01:40:42.154
and you blow into it,

01:40:42.154 --> 01:40:44.821
you can blow in and out.
- Right.

01:40:45.909 --> 01:40:48.659
- To create sort of a very, very-

01:40:49.531 --> 01:40:50.364
- Yeah, this thing, this thing,

01:40:50.364 --> 01:40:51.518
have you seen this thing?

01:40:51.518 --> 01:40:52.673
- Oh, I've seen that, yeah, yeah.

01:40:52.673 --> 01:40:55.223
- Oh, I have seen this.
- Yeah, yeah.

01:40:55.223 --> 01:40:56.359
- So it's a very complex,

01:40:56.359 --> 01:40:59.590
very beautiful sort of cluster of notes

01:40:59.590 --> 01:41:01.744
that create a very, very
dense, very specific,

01:41:01.744 --> 01:41:02.577
oh and all these chords

01:41:02.577 --> 01:41:03.830
have very, very, very special meaning.

01:41:03.830 --> 01:41:06.463
So first off, obviously,
you know, as an Australian,

01:41:06.463 --> 01:41:09.313
I really, really wanted to
be very careful about that

01:41:09.313 --> 01:41:11.627
and you know, there's been,

01:41:11.627 --> 01:41:13.127
I mean, there's always a bit
of an attitude in Australia

